<section xml:id="notes-week-06w">
  <title>Week 6: Multiple integrals; vector calculus</title>

<subsection>
<title>Double Integrals and Signed Volume</title>

<p>
The definite integral of a function of one variable measures signed area.
For functions of two variables, a double integral measures signed volume.
</p>

<definition>
<p>

For a function <m>f(x,y)</m> defined on a region <m>R</m>,

<me>
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA
</me>

represents the accumulated value of <m>f</m> over the region.

</p>
</definition>

subsection
<image width="50%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(-1.5,-1.5) node[right]{$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2,0) node[left]{$y$};
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3) node[above]{$z$};

\draw (1,-.5) ellipse (1.3 and .7);
\node at (1,-.5){$R$};

\draw[thick]
(.5,1)--(2,2)--(1.5,3)--(-.5,2)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<caption>
A double integral accumulates values over a planar region.
</caption>

subsection

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Iterated Integrals</title>

<p>
Most double integrals you will encounter are evaluated as repeated one-variable integrals.
</p>

<theorem>

<me>
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA
=
\int_a^b\int_{c(x)}^{d(x)} f(x,y)\,dy\,dx.
</me>

</theorem>

<example>

<statement>

Evaluate

<me>
\int_0^1\int_0^2 (x+y)\,dy\,dx.
</me>

</statement>
<solution>
  <me>
\int_0^1\int_0^2 (x+y)\,dy\,dx = \int_0^1 \left[xy + \frac{y^2}{2}\right]_0^2 dx =
</me>
  
<me>
= \int_0^1 (2x + 2) dx = \left[x^2 + 2x\right]_0^1 = 3.
</me>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Changing the Order of Integration</title>

<p>
Sometimes reversing the order of integration greatly simplifies a problem.
</p>

<activity>

Sketch the region described by

<me>
\int_0^1
\int_x^1
f(x,y)\,dy\,dx.
</me>

Rewrite the integral with the order reversed.
<solution>
  <image width="50%">
    <shortdescription></shortdescription>
    <latex-image>
      \begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (-.5,0)--(1.5,0);
\draw[->] (0,-.5)--(0,1.5);
\draw[thick,fill=blue!20] (0,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle;
\draw[->,thick,blue] (0,0.5)--(1,0.5);
\node at (1.5,-0.2){$x$};
\node at (-0.2,1.5){$y$};

\draw[->] (2.5,-0.5)--(2.5,1.5);
\draw[->] (2,0)--(4,0);
\draw[thick,fill=red!20] (2.5,0)--(3.5,1)--(2.5,1)--cycle;
\draw[->,thick,red] (3,0)--(3,1.5);
\node at (4,-.2){$x$};
\node at (2.3,1.5){$y$};
      \end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
  </image>
  <p>
The region is a triangle with vertices at (0,0), (1,1), and (0,1). The integral can be rewritten as
<md>
 \int_0^1
\int_x^1
f(x,y)\,dy\,dx=\int_0^1
\int_0^y
f(x,y)\,dx\,dy.
</md>

  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Applications</title>

<definition>

Average value of a function:

<me>
f_{avg}
=
\frac{1}{A(R)}
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA.
</me>

</definition>

<example>
<p>
  
Suppose <m>f(x,y)</m> represents temperature distribution over a metal plate.
Interpret

<me>
\frac{1}{A(R)}
\iint_Rf\,dA.
</me>

</p>
</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Polar Coordinates</title>

<p>
Circular regions are often easier to describe using polar coordinates.
</p>

<definition>

<me>
x=r\cos\theta,
\qquad
y=r\sin\theta.
</me>

</definition>

<theorem>

<me>
dA=r\,dr\,d\theta.
</me>

</theorem>

subsection

<image width="40%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}

\draw[->] (-2,0)--(2,0);
\draw[->] (0,-2)--(0,2);

\draw (0,0) circle (1.4);

\draw[thick,->] (0,0)--(1,.9);

\draw (.5,0) arc (0:42:.5);

\node at (.8,.25){$\theta$};
\node at (.7,.8){$r$};
\node at (1.5,1.5){$x$};
\node at (-1.5,1.5){$y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

subsection

<example>
<p>
Evaluate

<me>
\iint_R (x^2+y^2)\,dA
</me>

where <m>R</m> is the unit disk.

</p>
<solution>
  <p>
    In polar coordinates, <m>x = r\cos\theta</m> and <m>y = r\sin\theta</m>, so <m>x^2 + y^2 = r^2</m>. The integral becomes
    <me>
      \iint_R (x^2+y^2)\,dA = \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^1 r^2 \cdot r\,dr\,d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^1 r^3\,dr\,d\theta.
    </me>
    Evaluating the inner integral,
    <me>
      \int_0^1 r^3\,dr = \left[\frac{r^4}{4}\right]_0^1 = \frac{1}{4}.
    </me>
    Therefore,
    <me>
      \int_0^{2\pi} \frac{1}{4}\,d\theta = \frac{1}{4} \cdot 2\pi = \frac{\pi}{2}.
    </me>
  </p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Vector Fields</title>

<p>
A vector field assigns a vector to every point in a region.
</p>

<definition>
<p>
<me>
\mathbf F(x,y)
=
\langle P(x,y),Q(x,y)\rangle.
</me>
is a vector-function of two variables with two components.</p>
</definition>

<example>
<p>
Interpret and graph the vector field

<me>
\mathbf F(x,y)=\langle -y,x\rangle.
</me>
</p>
<solution>
  <p>
    The vector field <m>\mathbf F(x,y)=\langle -y,x\rangle</m> represents a rotational flow around the origin. At any point <m>(x,y)</m>, the vector points in the direction of the tangent to the circle centered at the origin passing through that point.
  </p>
  <image>
  <latex-image>
      \begin{tikzpicture}
        \draw[->,thick] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right]{$x$};
        \draw[->,thick] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above]{$y$};
        \foreach \x in {-2,-1,0,1,2} {
          \foreach \y in {-2,-1,0,1,2} {
            \draw[->,blue] (\x,\y) -- ++(-\y,\x);
          }
        } 
      \end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
    
  </image>
</solution>
</example>


</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>The Gradient</title>

<p>
The gradient links scalar functions and vector fields.
</p>

<me>
\nabla f
=
\left\langle
f_x,
f_y
\right\rangle.
</me>

<theorem>

<p>The gradient points in the direction of greatest increase.
</p>
</theorem>

<example>
<p>
Find the gradient of

<me>
f(x,y)=x^2+y^2.
</me>
</p>
<solution>
  <p>
    The gradient is computed as follows:
    <me>
      \nabla f = \left\langle \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \right\rangle = \left\langle 2x, 2y \right\rangle.
    </me>
    Therefore, the gradient vector at any point <m>(x,y)</m> is <m>\langle 2x, 2y \rangle</m>, which points radially outward from the origin, indicating the direction of greatest increase of the function <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2</m>.
  </p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Divergence</title>

<p>
Divergence measures expansion or compression of a vector field.
</p>

<definition>
<p>Divergence is defined as
<me>
Div(\mathbf F) = \nabla\cdot \mathbf F
=
\frac{\partial P}{\partial x}
+
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial y}.
</me>
</p>
</definition>


<sidebyside>



<image width="40%">
<shortdescription>Positive divergence:
vectors spread outward.</shortdescription>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(1,.5);
\draw[->] (0,0.5)--(.5,1.5);
\draw[->] (0,1)--(.5,1.7);
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>



<image width="40%">
<shortdescription>Negative divergence:
vectors move inward.</shortdescription>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(-1,-.5);
\draw[->] (-1,-.5)--(-.5,-1.5);
\draw[->] (-.5,-1.5)--(.5,-1);    
\draw[->] (.5,-1)--(1,-.5);
\draw[->] (1,-.5)--(1.5,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(1,.5);
\draw[->] (1,.5)--(.5,1.5);
\draw[->] (.5,1.5)--(-.5,1);
\draw[->] (-.5,1)--(-1,.5);
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>


</sidebyside>


<example>

Compute the divergence of

<me>
\mathbf F
=
\langle x,y\rangle.
</me>

</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Curl</title>

<p>
Curl measures local rotation.
</p>

<definition>

For a planar vector field,

<me>
\operatorname{curl}(\mathbf F)
=
\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}
-
\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}.
</me>

</definition>

<image width="45%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}

\draw[->] (0,0)--(1,.5);
\draw[->] (1,.5)--(.5,1.5);
\draw[->] (.5,1.5)--(-.5,1);
\draw[->] (-.5,1)--(0,0);

\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>


<example>

Find the curl of

<me>
\mathbf F
=
\langle -y,x\rangle.
</me>

</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Line Integrals</title>

<p>
A line integral accumulates values along a curve.
</p>

<definition>

<me>
\int_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r.
</me>

</definition>

<p>
Physical interpretation:
work done by a force field.
</p>

<example>

Compute the work done by

<me>
\mathbf F=\langle 1,2\rangle
</me>

along a straight path.

</example>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Conservative Vector Fields</title>

<definition>

A vector field is conservative if

<me>
\mathbf F=\nabla f
</me>

for some potential function <m>f</m>.

</definition>

<theorem>

For a conservative field,

<me>
\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
=
f(B)-f(A).
</me>

</theorem>

<activity>

Find a potential function for

<me>
\mathbf F=\langle 2x,2y\rangle.
</me>

</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection>

<title>Big Picture</title>


<image width="60%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=3cm]

\node (f) {$f$};

\node[right of=f] (g) {$\nabla f$};

\node[right of=g] (d) {$\nabla\cdot \mathbf F$};

\node[below of=g] (c) {$\mathrm{curl}(\mathbf F)$};

\draw[->] (f)--(g);
\draw[->] (g)--(d);
\draw[->] (g)--(c);

\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</subsection>



</section>
